DATASET LICENSE
===============

Volga-IT 2026 semifinal, "Artificial Intelligence and Data Analysis".
Russian license plate detection and recognition dataset.

The competition rules state that the submitted dataset will be published under
CC BY 4.0 and may be used and published by the organizers with attribution.
The dataset is therefore assembled so that it can be released on those terms in
full. This is a hard constraint on what may be included; see section 2.


1. OUR OWN CONTRIBUTIONS
------------------------

The following were produced by this project, and are released under the
Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license (CC BY 4.0),
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ , to the extent that we hold the
rights necessary to do so:

  - all annotations in meta.csv and in labels/
  - all synthetic images under images/synthetic/, and the generator output
    they were produced from
  - the synthetic image generator under generator/
  - the documentation in this directory

Attribution: "Volga-IT 2026 license plate dataset".

The qualification "to the extent that we hold the necessary rights" is
deliberate. Where a synthetic image is composited onto a third-party
background, or a generator asset (font, template, texture) comes from a third
party, our CC BY 4.0 grant covers only our own contribution. The underlying
asset keeps its own terms, which must themselves permit redistribution under
this dataset's license; those assets are registered in
../docs/data_sources.md like any other source.


2. THIRD-PARTY IMAGES
---------------------

This license does NOT, and cannot, alter the terms of any third-party
material. Our CC BY 4.0 grant in section 1 applies solely to what we made. No
grant we make here extends to an image we did not create.

Because the dataset as a whole is published under CC BY 4.0, a third-party
image may be included ONLY IF its own terms permit that publication. In
practice an image qualifies only when its license permits all of:

  - redistribution as part of a published dataset,
  - commercial use,
  - modification (we crop, resize and blur faces),

with attribution as the only condition. Public-domain and CC0 material, plain
CC BY material, and our own photographs qualify. The following do NOT, and are
excluded without exception:

  - NonCommercial (NC) terms,
  - NoDerivatives (ND) terms,
  - ShareAlike (SA) terms, which permit redistribution but forbid the
    relicensing under plain CC BY that publication requires,
  - "free to use, no redistribution" terms, however permissive otherwise,
  - any license whose terms are unclear or could not be verified.

Per-image provenance is recorded in meta.csv: the `license` column holds the
exact license, and the `source` column holds a source_id registered in
../docs/data_sources.md, which records the redistribution check and the date it
was performed. scripts/validate_dataset_local.py enforces this mechanically:
an NC, ND or SA license is a validation error, and an unrecognised license is a
warning that must be resolved by a person before submission.

Required attributions for included images are listed in ../docs/data_sources.md
and are reproduced wherever the dataset is redistributed.


2a. INCLUDED THIRD-PARTY IMAGES
-------------------------------

Wikimedia Commons, curated per file (source_id `wikimedia_commons_curated`).
13 photographs, acquired 2026-09-22 and promoted after a human review on the
same day. Each was selected individually; the rights sit on each file, not on
the collection, so each photograph keeps its own license and its own credit.

We do NOT relicense these photographs. Our CC BY 4.0 grant in section 1 covers
our annotations, not the images below. Each image is included because its own
license already permits redistribution, commercial use and modification as part
of a dataset published under CC BY 4.0 -- which CC0 and CC BY 4.0 both do.

CC0 1.0 files carry no legal attribution requirement. We credit their authors
anyway: the requirement is absent, the courtesy is not.

Faces were blurred in 5 of these 13 images before promotion. Blurring does not
change where an image came from: each row keeps its original `source` and
`license`, and the per-image privacy action is recorded in the intake records
under data/real_staging/.

Full per-file provenance -- original URL, file page, author, exact license,
license URL, required attribution, acquisition timestamp and SHA-256 -- is in
data/real_staging/incoming/wikimedia_commons_curated/acquisition_record.csv.

